OEKG:89b0f42b-6c05-5eea-21e2-460704fa10c0 a OEO:OEO_00020227 ;
    RDFS:label "Wege zu einem klimaneutralen Energiesystem - Die deutsche Energiewende im Kontext gesellschaftlicher Verhaltensweisen"^^XSD:string ;
    OBO:BFO_0000051 <https://openenergyplatform.org/ontology/oekg/models/34>,
        <https://openenergyplatform.org/ontology/oekg/publication/cc99a4ce-aae8-c98a-184b-f44180720354>,
        <https://openenergyplatform.org/ontology/oekg/scenario/1a0e48fa-1d93-bef8-8497-5607632e7093>,
        <https://openenergyplatform.org/ontology/oekg/scenario/605c3865-0f27-5ce1-f8a9-73d64ba33e4b>,
        <https://openenergyplatform.org/ontology/oekg/scenario/665cdfaf-b0d2-fc51-7110-1d5bbfdf8824>,
        <https://openenergyplatform.org/ontology/oekg/scenario/c6885c79-4d75-d77b-1757-25263a8647da> ;
    DC:abstract """Following the German Bundestag's decision on the Climate Protection Act (KSG) in June 2021, with the stricter target of greenhouse gas neutrality for Germany in 2045, the Fraunhofer Institute for Solar Energy Systems ISE has completely recalculated its study "Pathways to a climate-neutral energy system" using its REMod energy system model. The institute works with four scenarios to analyse the effects of social trends on achieving the climate targets. In all scenarios, the reduction targets can still be achieved by 2045, but along different paths and at different costs.

For the new study, the new stricter interim targets of the KSG of -65 per cent in 2030 and -88 per cent in 2040 as well as the target of greenhouse gas neutrality of energy-related CO2 emissions in 2045 were implemented as boundary conditions in REMod.

The study "Paths to a climate-neutral energy system - The German energy transition in the context of social behaviour" examines development paths of the German energy system that lead to a reduction in energy-related CO₂ emissions of between 95 and 100 percent by 2050. Achieving these climate protection targets in the energy supply based on renewable energies is therefore feasible from a technical and systemic perspective. However, social behaviour is proving to be a decisive factor for the path that the energy transition takes and for the costs of the system transformation.

In this study, the researchers look at the course, technical feasibility and costs of the energy transition in the context of various developments in social behaviour and attitudes. To this end, they calculated four main scenarios:

- Persistence scenario (strong resistance to the use of new technologies in the private sector)
- Inacceptance scenario (strong resistance to the expansion of large infrastructures) 
- Sufficiency scenario (changes in social behaviour significantly reduce energy consumption)
- Reference scenario (without other boundary conditions that promote or hinder the achievement of targets)

The REMod (Renewable Energy Model) energy system model developed at Fraunhofer ISE was used to simulate and optimise the scenarios."""^^XSD:string ;
    DC:acronym "ISE-2045"^^XSD:string ;
    OEO:OEO_00000510 OEKG:e587f816-9070-7dbb-6017-812c30027eaa ;
